11. Interview Explanation (30–60 sec)

> The key observation is that the score of a path is the minimum edge on that path. Since the problem allows revisiting cities and roads, we can traverse anywhere inside the connected component containing city 1 before reaching city n. Therefore, the answer is simply the smallest edge weight in that connected component. We can find it by performing a DFS/BFS from city 1 while tracking the minimum edge, which runs in O(V + E) time. Another optimal solution uses Disjoint Set Union (DSU) to identify the connected component and then scans its edges.




---

12. Key Takeaways

The shortest path is not required.

Revisiting roads changes the problem significantly.

Only the connected component of city 1 matters.

DFS, BFS, and DSU all solve the problem efficiently.

The optimal time complexity is O(V + E) (or O(E·α(N)) with DSU).



---

13. Common Mistakes

❌ Using Dijkstra's algorithm because the problem is not asking for the shortest path.

❌ Searching all possible paths, leading to exponential time.

❌ Assuming the path must be simple (without revisiting nodes).

❌ Forgetting that revisiting roads allows including any edge in the connected component.

❌ Tracking path length instead of the minimum edge weight.



